Reason:
The loop clears the COPY_MARKER flag before calling update_marker_trace(). In update_marker_trace(), the condition “if (list_empty(&tr->marker_list))” returns false because the flag was already cleared, even though the trace_array descriptor is still on the marker_copies list. Consequently, the function returns false, synchronize_rcu() is skipped, and the list entry is never removed – leading to a use‑after‑free when RCU readers traverse the list.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (update_marker_trace(tr, 0))
    synchronize_rcu();
for (i = 0; i < TRACE_FLAGS_MAX_SIZE; i++) {
    if ((1ULL << i) & ZEROED_TRACE_FLAGS)
        set_tracer_flag(tr, 1ULL << i, 0);
}
if (!(tr->trace_flags & TRACE_ITER(COPY_MARKER)))
    return false;
list_del_rcu(&tr->marker_list);
Reason:
The loop that clears flags is moved after update_marker_trace(), so the COPY_MARKER flag remains set when update_marker_trace() is called. Inside update_marker_trace(), the check now uses the flag (instead of list_empty) and correctly detects that the entry should be removed. It calls list_del_rcu() and returns true, triggering synchronize_rcu(). This ensures the trace_array descriptor is safely removed from the RCU‑protected list before the instance is freed, eliminating the UAF bug.

